-- Explicit `Basics.eq` on a `type` that wraps a schema endpoint
-- whose `via` target holds a function is `not_equatable` (checker-v2.md
-- §11.4), in its own module and in an
-- importer. The structural gate of a `type` is "no function reachable from
-- its payloads", through its module's endpoints and their `via` targets —
-- the same fact `==` reads off the derived contexts — and an importer reads
-- it from the record. It is not the table-build bit ("no function in its
-- declaration"), which cannot see a `via` target and would refuse `==` on
-- `Holds` while accepting `Basics.eq` on it: two answers to one question.
--
--
-- (2026-10-02: `Basics.eq` and `Basics.neq` ask for `eq` through a
-- `where` clause, as `==` does — static-dispatch-spike.md §3.1, amended —
-- and no longer through the `equatable` marker. The refusal stands; it is
-- reached the way a `where` clause's is.)
import B


viaEq : Bool
viaEq = B.mk 1 == B.mk 2


viaBasics : Bool
viaBasics = Basics.eq (B.mk 1) (B.mk 2)
